  <NugetDependency Id="IBwmTF8slKzsYSW1d5MNZY" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="PLJfsysP5G7maogtHcEIgj">
    <Canvas Id="AsgVkRa0ZugbmU7C0WJYxr" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="JU24nlfZ5uZ1KrZ0WKgXA6">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="E4HPkWK7IbrLXovL1ED45Z">
        <Canvas Id="QBKvPVf5uRTtKQYGfdRFDU" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="NkXosAWdZtRIuhKEFoSVvM" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1560" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Walk across a mountain.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;TWO SWITCHES, BOTH OFF WHEN YOU ARRIVE. Basemap fetches OpenStreetMap tiles; Elevation fetches&#xD;&#xA;terrain tiles. Opening a patch is not the same as agreeing to network traffic, so you switch them&#xD;&#xA;on. Then move the mouse over Fuji.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE NUMBER BESIDE YOUR CURSOR IS THE HEIGHT OF THE GROUND THERE, IN METRES. Walk up the slope&#xD;&#xA;from the lake and watch it climb. At the crater it reads about 3740. Fuji is 3776 m - the gap is&#xD;&#xA;the chapter-12 lesson, alive: a tile pixel at this zoom is about 76 m across, and it holds ONE&#xD;&#xA;number for that whole square, so the summit is averaged with its shoulders.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM. Not from a file, not from a special library - from a picture.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Your cursor      -&gt;  longitude, latitude                         (ScreenToWorld, chapter 08)&#xD;&#xA;  lon, lat         -&gt;  which tile, and which pixel inside it        (chapter 10's Mercator formula, again)&#xD;&#xA;  tile             -&gt;  one PNG from the internet, 256 x 256          (HTTPGet, as in Live earthquakes)&#xD;&#xA;  pixel            -&gt;  its red, green and blue                       (Pipet)&#xD;&#xA;  R, G, B          -&gt;  metres                                        (one line of arithmetic)&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE PICTURE IS NOT A PICTURE. Look at the TILE readout - or open the URL in a browser. It is&#xD;&#xA;pink-green noise. Nobody could look at it and see a mountain, because the colours are not colours:&#xD;&#xA;they are a 24-bit number split across three 8-bit channels. Red holds 256s, green holds ones,&#xD;&#xA;blue holds 1/256ths, and the whole thing is offset by 32768 so the sea floor can be negative:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  height = (R x 256 + G + B / 256) - 32768&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That formula IS the metadata. Without it the pixel is a colour. With it, the pixel is a height.&#xD;&#xA;Chapter 12 said a value means nothing without its contract; here is a contract, and it fits in&#xD;&#xA;one line, and the PNG does not carry it - the documentation does. Change one constant and&#xD;&#xA;every mountain on earth is wrong by the same amount, silently.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHY THIS IS POSSIBLE WITHOUT ANY NEW NODE. A DEM does not have to arrive as a GeoTIFF. It&#xD;&#xA;arrives every day as ordinary map tiles, in the same {z}/{x}/{y} scheme as the basemap, in the&#xD;&#xA;same Web Mercator - and everything needed to read one was already here: an HTTP fetch, an image&#xD;&#xA;decoder, a pixel picker. The only thing that had to be written down was the arithmetic.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ONE FETCH PER TILE, NEVER PER FRAME. Watch TILE while you move: the request fires only when the&#xD;&#xA;x/y in the URL actually change, through the Changed node. Move within a tile and nothing is&#xD;&#xA;fetched; the pixel is re-read from the picture already in hand. A patch that fetched on every&#xD;&#xA;frame would make sixty requests a second for the same file, and that is the difference between a&#xD;&#xA;patch and an abuse report.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Drag the map out to sea. The number goes to 0, or slightly below - Terrarium's sea is the&#xD;&#xA;  bathymetry where it has it. Drag to Kawaguchi-ko and read the lake's surface: about 830 m.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Change the zoom pad from 11 to 13 - and the TILES PER SIDE pad from 2048 to 8192 to match.&#xD;&#xA;  The crater reads closer to 3776 now: smaller pixels, less averaging. That is resolution.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Break the contract on purpose: change the 32768 to 32000. Every height is now 768 m too high&#xD;&#xA;  and nothing anywhere will tell you. That is what a wrong metadata line does to a dataset.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  DEM                digital elevation model - a raster whose value is height&#xD;&#xA;  terrain tile       a DEM cut into the same z/x/y squares as a web basemap&#xD;&#xA;  Terrarium          one particular way of packing a height into R, G, B. Mapbox Terrain-RGB is&#xD;&#xA;                     another, with a different offset and scale - same idea, different contract&#xD;&#xA;  encoding           the contract that turns channel values back into a quantity&#xD;&#xA;  nearest sampling   reading the one pixel under the cursor. Bilinear would blend four; both are&#xD;&#xA;                     choices, and this patch makes the simpler one&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  AND THE ATTRIBUTION, which is not optional: these tiles are a mosaic of national open datasets&#xD;&#xA;  and the terms require credit for analysis as well as display. It is on the screen, bottom left,&#xD;&#xA;  and in this pack's THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  You have read data out of a texture before - a height map into a vertex shader, a lookup table&#xD;&#xA;  in a PNG, positions baked into pixels. This is that, except the texture is the earth and the&#xD;&#xA;  contract for decoding it was written by someone else.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 12              why a pixel value is not a height until you know its contract&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 10              the Mercator arithmetic this patch reuses to find the tile&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt A mountain        the same mountain, as objects - peaks, trails, huts">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <!-- ===== basemap ===== -->
          <Pad Id="AKjiMOpoNvhgDrGOveUzsN" Comment="OpenStreetMap" Bounds="-50,-115,430,15" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png">
